From c461b798f301a310ed8bb2a5768a193aecb6b2d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: li-yiyang <yiyang.li.11pro@outlook.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 23:24:01 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sdhci-msm: support the BayHub BH201 SD retimer

The microSD slot on the Lenovo Y700 Gen 5 (TB323FU) has a BayHub BH201
retimer between the controller and the card. The retimer has its own TX
phase for SDR104, and the power-on phase leaves some cards with data CRC
errors at 202 MHz. The host's tuning only moves the controller's sampling
point, so it passes while reads still fail.

Configure the retimer from init_card as the vendor driver does (a
configuration block written with CMD24 while the card is in standby,
which the card ignores), and calibrate the 200 MHz phase from
execute_tuning: try two known phases first, then sweep all 121 if they
fail. A phase is accepted after it reads LBA0 back three times and 4 MiB
without a CRC error on two fresh attaches in a row; each trial fails the
attach so that the core re-initialises the card with the next phase.
Depending on the card, a known phase passes in under a second or the
sweep takes under a minute.

Calibration is off by default: the slot runs at 100 MHz, where SDR104
needs no calibration, and the retimer is left alone. The module
parameter sd_retimer_o2.calibrate turns it on from the next card
insertion or rebind. If no phase passes, the card stays at 100 MHz. If a
calibrated phase stops reading after a resume or a retune, that attach
is lowered to 100 MHz rather than failed, since failing it could remove
the block device under a mounted root; the next attach also runs at
100 MHz and the one after re-checks the phase. The card state is checked
with CMD13 before the configuration writes, and the driver stops using
the retimer if LBA0 reads back different from the previous attach.

Based on sdhci-bayhub.c by Bayhub Technologies. The resume clamp and the
one attach at 100 MHz after it are from li-yiyang's follow-up patches.

Signed-off-by: li-yiyang <yiyang.li.11pro@outlook.com>
Co-developed-by: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
---
